  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Compliance automation platforms connect to hundreds of cloud services to collect evidence automatically. Master Vanta, Drata, and Secureframe integration architecture and building a compliance program that runs itself.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Compliance Automation Platforms — Vanta, Drata, Secureframe & Evidence Collection at Scale',
      'description', 'Compliance automation platforms connect to hundreds of cloud services to collect evidence automatically. Master Vanta, Drata, Secureframe, and building a compliance program that runs itself.',
      'transcript', 'Compliance automation platforms have transformed the economics of enterprise compliance. Before these platforms existed, achieving SOC 2 Type II or ISO 27001 certification required months of manual evidence collection — security teams downloading reports, taking screenshots, organizing files, and manually cross-referencing evidence to controls. The largest companies employed dedicated compliance teams of 5–15 people to manage this. Compliance automation compresses this effort by 80% through direct API integration with the systems that generate evidence.

How compliance automation works: the platform integrates with your cloud environments, developer tools, HR systems, and SaaS applications via OAuth, API keys, or native connectors. For each integration, the platform continuously collects configuration state and activity data, mapping it to specific compliance controls. AWS integration: the platform queries IAM configurations (checking MFA enforcement, password policy, access key age), CloudTrail settings (checking that logging is enabled in all regions), security group configurations (checking for overpermissive rules), and S3 bucket policies (checking for public access). GitHub integration: the platform checks branch protection settings (are PRs required? is code review enforced?), checks SAST tool integration in CI pipelines, and checks for secrets in repositories. Okta integration: the platform pulls MFA enrollment status for all users, checks session timeout policies, and exports authentication logs.

Vanta is the market leader in compliance automation for startup and mid-market companies. Integrates with 200+ tools across cloud providers, identity providers, developer tools, HR systems, and communication platforms. Primary frameworks: SOC 2, ISO 27001, HIPAA, GDPR, PCI DSS, NIST CSF. Vanta''s employee management feature tracks security training completion, laptop enrollment (ensuring all employee devices are managed and meet security requirements), and policy acknowledgment — generating the HR-related evidence that auditors request for CC1 and CC6 controls. Vanta provides an auditor access portal: instead of a PBC list request process, the auditor receives direct read-only access to the Vanta platform where all evidence is organized by control and framework.

Drata: similar to Vanta in breadth, differentiated by stronger security questionnaire automation (automatically populating security questionnaires like SIG and CAIQ from Drata''s evidence base), strong policy management (built-in policy library with pre-written templates), and more sophisticated risk management integration. Particularly strong for organizations that need to respond to frequent customer security questionnaires as part of their sales process.

Secureframe: strong for US government and regulated industry compliance frameworks — FedRAMP, CMMC (Cybersecurity Maturity Model Certification for DoD contractors), NIST SP 800-171, in addition to commercial frameworks. If your organization sells to US federal agencies, Secureframe''s FedRAMP support is best-in-class.

Evidence quality management: compliance automation collects evidence continuously, but the quality of that evidence depends on the underlying controls being properly configured. A Vanta integration that shows "MFA enabled for 47/150 users" is evidence — but it is evidence of a compliance gap, not compliance. The platform makes gaps visible so they can be remediated before the audit. Build a weekly evidence review process: review all red (failing) and yellow (at-risk) controls, assign remediation owners and deadlines, and track progress to audit-readiness.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 1;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'SOAR automates the repetitive tasks that exhaust SOC analysts. Master playbook design, orchestration architecture, response action libraries, and building automations that cut MTTR from hours to minutes.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'SOAR Architecture — Playbook Design, Orchestration & Automated Response',
      'description', 'SOAR automates the repetitive tasks that exhaust SOC analysts. Master playbook design, orchestration architecture, response action libraries, and building automations that cut MTTR from hours to minutes.',
      'transcript', 'Security Orchestration, Automation and Response (SOAR) platforms automate the repetitive, time-consuming tasks that consume SOC analyst capacity without requiring analytical judgment. When a Tier 1 analyst spends 20 minutes per alert doing the same enrichment and triage steps — looking up IPs in VirusTotal, checking asset ownership in the CMDB, querying previous tickets for the same user, updating the ticket with findings — SOAR executes those steps in 30 seconds, allowing the analyst to focus exclusively on the analytical judgment that automation cannot replace.

SOAR architecture: the platform connects to every security tool in the environment via APIs and provides a visual playbook editor for defining automated workflows. Core components: Integrations (pre-built connectors to SIEM platforms, EDR tools, threat intelligence platforms, ticketing systems, identity providers, firewalls, and cloud environments), Playbooks (visual workflow definitions using trigger-condition-action logic), Cases (incident management workspace where playbook outputs are consolidated for analyst review), and Dashboards (metrics on playbook execution, alert volume, response times, and analyst workload).

Playbook design principles: start with the highest-volume alert types (phishing alerts, malware detections, suspicious login alerts), define the enrichment steps that every alert of that type requires, identify which actions can be taken automatically versus which require analyst approval, and define the escalation path and notification logic. A well-designed playbook handles 80% of alert instances completely automatically, with the remaining 20% automatically enriched and presented to an analyst with all the context needed to make a decision in under 5 minutes.

Example phishing alert playbook: Trigger: SIEM alert "Suspicious email with malicious URL clicked by user". Step 1 (Enrichment, automated): extract URL from alert, submit to VirusTotal, check URL reputation in proxy logs, check if user has clicked similar URLs in the past 30 days, retrieve user profile from HR system (role, department, manager). Step 2 (Condition branch): if VirusTotal score > 70 AND proxy logs show the URL was accessed → HIGH RISK branch. If VirusTotal score < 30 AND no proxy access → LOW RISK branch. Step 3a (HIGH RISK automated actions): disable user account in Active Directory, revoke active sessions in Okta, isolate user laptop via EDR API, create P1 incident ticket with all enrichment context, notify manager and security team via Slack and PagerDuty. Step 3b (LOW RISK automated actions): add URL to proxy blocklist, create P3 ticket for analyst review, send security awareness reminder email to user.

SOAR platform selection: Splunk SOAR (formerly Phantom) — most extensive integration library (450+ apps), best for organizations already using Splunk SIEM. Palo Alto XSOAR (formerly Demisto) — strong for organizations in the Palo Alto ecosystem, powerful Python scripting for custom integrations. Swimlane — strongest for high-volume SOC environments, excellent for case management and metrics. Microsoft Sentinel Logic Apps — native SOAR capability built into Sentinel, best for Microsoft-centric environments without a dedicated SOAR budget.

Measuring SOAR impact: MTTR (Mean Time to Respond) before and after SOAR deployment is the primary metric. Leading implementations report MTTR reduction from 4–6 hours to 15–30 minutes for common alert types. Analyst capacity: how many alerts can each analyst handle effectively? SOAR implementations typically increase effective analyst capacity by 3–5x for automated alert types. False positive reduction: SOAR-driven enrichment allows more sophisticated triage, reducing false positive rates as analysts have more context for each decision.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 2;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Policy as code makes security guardrails programmable, versioned, and enforceable. Master Open Policy Agent (OPA), the Rego language, and implementing policy enforcement across Kubernetes, Terraform, and API authorization.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Policy Validation as Code — Open Policy Agent (OPA) & Rego Language',
      'description', 'Policy as code makes security guardrails programmable and enforceable. Master Open Policy Agent, Rego language, Kubernetes admission control, Terraform validation, and API authorization.',
      'transcript', 'Policy as Code is the practice of expressing security and compliance policies as machine-executable code, enforcing them programmatically rather than through manual review processes. Instead of a security policy document that says "no container should run as root" and relying on developers to read and follow it, Policy as Code expresses that requirement as code that automatically blocks any container configured to run as root from being deployed — regardless of whether the developer read the policy.

Open Policy Agent (OPA) is the CNCF-graduated standard for policy as code, providing a unified engine for policy enforcement across diverse systems. OPA decouples policy decisions from policy enforcement points: the application or infrastructure component sends a query to OPA (should this request be allowed?), OPA evaluates the query against the loaded policies and data, and returns an allow/deny decision. The enforcement point acts on the decision. This architecture enables: centralized policy management (all policies defined in one place), language-agnostic enforcement (OPA can enforce policies for Kubernetes, Terraform, REST APIs, gRPC services, and any system that can make an HTTP call), and testable policies (policies are code that can be unit-tested).

Rego is OPA''s purpose-built policy language. Rego policies define rules: conditions that must be true for a policy to allow or deny an action. A Kubernetes admission policy in Rego: package kubernetes.admission. deny[msg] { input.request.kind.kind == "Pod" ; container := input.request.object.spec.containers[_] ; container.securityContext.runAsUser == 0 ; msg := sprintf("Container %v must not run as root (runAsUser 0)", [container.name]) }. This policy evaluates every Pod creation or modification request, checks each container''s security context, and denies any that specify runAsUser 0. The same pattern applies to checking for missing resource limits, disallowed image registries, missing liveness probes, or any other organizational security standard.

Kubernetes admission control with OPA/Gatekeeper: OPA Gatekeeper is the Kubernetes-native integration for OPA. It implements the Kubernetes Admission Controller webhook pattern: every resource creation, modification, or deletion request passes through Gatekeeper before being persisted to etcd. Gatekeeper evaluates the request against all defined Constraint Templates (OPA policies in Kubernetes CRD format) and rejects requests that violate any active constraint. Policy library: the OPA library provides pre-built Gatekeeper policies for CIS Kubernetes Benchmark controls, PCI DSS requirements, and NIST SP 800-190 container security guidelines.

Terraform validation with OPA and Conftest: Conftest is a tool for writing policy-as-code tests against structured configuration files, including Terraform HCL, Kubernetes YAML, Dockerfile, and JSON/YAML configuration. Integrate Conftest into CI/CD pipelines to evaluate Terraform plan output against security policies before terraform apply is permitted. Policy examples: deny any S3 bucket with public access enabled, deny any security group with inbound rules permitting 0.0.0.0/0 on administrative ports (22, 3389), deny any RDS instance without encryption at rest, deny any IAM policy with a wildcard (*) resource. These checks run in the CI/CD pipeline in seconds, before any infrastructure is provisioned.

API authorization with OPA: OPA integrates with API gateways (Kong, Envoy, AWS API Gateway) to externalize authorization decisions. Instead of each microservice implementing its own authorization logic (with inconsistency across services), every API request is evaluated by OPA. The OPA policy receives the request context (user identity, JWT claims, requested resource, HTTP method, time of day) and returns an allow/deny decision based on organizational RBAC or ABAC policies. This centralizes authorization logic, makes it auditable, and enables organization-wide policy changes without code deployments.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 3;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Cloud infrastructure scanning tools find misconfigurations before they become breaches. Master Prowler, ScoutSuite, AWS Config, and Azure Policy for continuous cloud security posture management.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Cloud Infrastructure Scanning — Prowler, ScoutSuite, AWS Config & Azure Policy',
      'description', 'Cloud infrastructure scanning finds misconfigurations before they become breaches. Master Prowler, ScoutSuite, AWS Config, Azure Policy, and building automated cloud security assurance.',
      'transcript', 'Cloud infrastructure scanning tools audit your cloud environments against security best practices and compliance frameworks, identifying misconfigurations before attackers do. Unlike SIEM which detects threats after they occur, cloud scanning is preventive — it tells you about weaknesses in your current configuration so you can fix them before they are exploited.

Prowler is the most comprehensive open-source multi-cloud security scanning tool. Originally AWS-only, it now supports AWS, Azure, GCP, and Kubernetes. Prowler contains over 350 security checks mapped to security frameworks: CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark (v2.0 and v3.0), CIS Azure Foundations Benchmark, CIS GCP Foundations Benchmark, AWS Well-Architected Framework Security Pillar, SOC 2, ISO 27001 Annex A, HIPAA, NIST CSF, PCI DSS, and GDPR. Running Prowler produces a findings report categorized by severity (Critical, High, Medium, Low, Informational) with specific remediation guidance for each finding. Prowler integration patterns: run on a schedule (daily via Lambda/Cloud Function), output results to S3/Blob Storage, and import into your SIEM or compliance platform for tracking and trending. Cost: fully open-source, with Prowler Pro (commercial) providing a managed dashboard and compliance reporting.

ScoutSuite: multi-cloud security auditing tool from NCC Group. Focuses on a comprehensive configuration audit — querying every cloud service and presenting findings in an interactive HTML report that allows drill-down by service, severity, and finding type. ScoutSuite is particularly useful for one-time or periodic comprehensive audits of cloud environments, and for auditing cloud environments you do not manage (assessing a target''s cloud security posture during M&A due diligence or a third-party assessment).

AWS Config: the native AWS service for continuous configuration recording and compliance evaluation. Config records every configuration change for every AWS resource type (EC2 instances, security groups, S3 buckets, IAM roles, RDS instances, etc.) with a full history. Config Rules define compliance requirements: is S3 block public access enabled? Is CloudTrail enabled in all regions? Are EBS volumes encrypted? Config continuously evaluates resources against rules and marks non-compliant resources. Conformance Packs bundle multiple Config Rules into a single deployment: AWS provides managed conformance packs for CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark, PCI DSS, HIPAA, and NIST SP 800-53. Config findings feed into Security Hub for centralized visibility.

Azure Policy: the native Azure governance service for enforcing organizational standards. Policies define rules that Azure Resource Manager evaluates on every resource creation and modification request. Effects: Audit (log non-compliant resources without blocking), Deny (prevent creation or modification of non-compliant resources), DeployIfNotExists (automatically deploy a required configuration alongside a resource — e.g., deploy Defender for Servers whenever a VM is created). Policy Initiatives (bundled policy sets) implement regulatory compliance: the Azure Security Benchmark initiative contains 180+ policies aligned to Microsoft''s security best practices. The NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5 initiative, SOC 2 initiative, and HIPAA/HITRUST initiative are pre-built by Microsoft. Compliance state is visible in the Azure Policy compliance dashboard — showing percentage of compliant resources by policy and by subscription.

Building a cloud security assurance program: integrate scanning tools into your CI/CD pipeline (IaC scanning with Checkov/tfsec at PR time, infrastructure scanning post-deployment), set SLAs for remediation by severity (Critical: 24 hours, High: 7 days, Medium: 30 days), report weekly on finding counts and trends, and track remediation progress against SLAs. The goal is a continuously shrinking finding backlog as new findings are addressed promptly and systemic issues are fixed at the IaC template level.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 4;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Python automation and cloud APIs can replace weeks of manual compliance work. Build evidence collection pipelines, automated access review reports, and security posture dashboards that run without human intervention.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Building Compliance Pipelines with Python, APIs & CI/CD Integration',
      'description', 'Python automation and cloud APIs replace weeks of manual compliance work. Build evidence collection pipelines, automated access review reports, and security posture dashboards.',
      'transcript', 'Compliance automation platforms handle most standard evidence collection, but every organization has bespoke systems, legacy applications, and unique compliance requirements that purpose-built platforms do not cover. Python scripting against cloud APIs fills these gaps — enabling custom compliance pipelines, automated evidence collection, and security posture reporting for any system that exposes an API.

Foundation: cloud SDKs. AWS: boto3 (the official AWS SDK for Python). Azure: azure-mgmt family of packages (azure-mgmt-compute, azure-mgmt-network, azure-mgmt-authorization, azure-identity). GCP: google-cloud Python SDK (google-cloud-iam, google-cloud-resourcemanager, google-cloud-logging). These SDKs provide programmatic access to every cloud service configuration — the same information that the AWS console shows, queryable in Python code.

Evidence collection pipeline pattern: a scheduled Lambda function (or Azure Function, or Cloud Run job) runs on a defined schedule (nightly, or more frequently for critical controls). The function queries cloud APIs for the configuration state of specific resources, formats the results as structured evidence (JSON or CSV with timestamps), uploads to a designated evidence bucket (S3, Azure Blob, GCS) with a dated folder structure, and optionally sends a summary report to the security team via email or Slack. For auditors, the evidence bucket provides timestamped, automated evidence for the review period — demonstrating that controls were in effect not just at audit time, but throughout the year.

AWS example: automated IAM access key age report. This script identifies all IAM access keys older than 90 days — a common compliance finding and a security risk (long-lived access keys are more likely to be compromised). Python code using boto3: list all IAM users, for each user list their access keys, filter keys older than 90 days, export the list with username, key ID, creation date, and last used date to CSV, upload to S3 with today''s date in the path, send a Slack notification listing key owners who need to rotate. This script runs weekly via a scheduled Lambda and generates weekly evidence that the access key age control is being monitored.

GitHub API compliance automation: GitHub''s REST and GraphQL APIs expose repository security settings, making automated compliance checks for source code security controls straightforward. Automated checks: branch protection enabled on all production branches (require PR reviews, require status checks, no force pushes), SAST tools (CodeQL or Semgrep) configured in CI workflows, secret scanning enabled at the organization level, Dependabot enabled for all repositories. Python using PyGithub: iterate all repositories in the organization, check each security setting, output a compliance report listing repositories that fail each check, send weekly email report to security team and repository owners.

Okta API compliance automation: MFA enrollment report for SOC 2 CC6.1. Using the Okta Management API: retrieve all active users, check each user''s MFA factor enrollment status, calculate MFA enrollment percentage, identify users enrolled in non-approved MFA methods (SMS OTP flagged for migration to TOTP or FIDO2), and generate a weekly report. Access review evidence: Okta''s Users API exports the full access review dataset needed for quarterly access certifications — user list with group memberships, application assignments, last login date, and account status — formatted for manager review.

CI/CD integration for compliance: security compliance checks embedded in CI/CD pipelines provide continuous evidence generation at deployment time. Every deployment triggers: infrastructure state validation (Prowler or Checkov scan of changed resources), dependency vulnerability check (Snyk or Dependabot report), container image scan (Trivy report), and policy compliance check (OPA/Conftest evaluation of Terraform plan). Results are archived in the CI/CD platform (GitHub Actions artifacts, Jenkins artifact storage) with a link in the deployment ticket — providing auditable evidence that security checks were performed for every production change.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 5;

-- ─── MODULE 16: Audit Readiness & Executive Communication ────────────────────

  SELECT id INTO v_ch FROM public.chapters
    WHERE course_id = v_cid ORDER BY order_index LIMIT 1 OFFSET 15;
