  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Internal auditing is the quality control mechanism for your security program. Master audit planning, evidence-based fieldwork, findings classification, corrective action tracking, and reporting that drives improvement.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Internal Audit Methodology — Planning, Fieldwork, Findings & Reporting',
      'description', 'Internal auditing is the quality control mechanism for your security program. Master audit planning, evidence-based fieldwork, findings classification, corrective action tracking, and driving improvement.',
      'transcript', 'Internal auditing is the systematic, independent examination of whether your security controls are designed correctly and operating effectively. It is the mechanism by which the organization catches problems before external auditors do, demonstrates continuous improvement to regulatory bodies and clients, and maintains the credibility of its compliance program between certification cycles.

Internal audit governance: ISO 19011 (Guidelines for Auditing Management Systems) is the standard for conducting internal audits of ISO management systems. The internal audit program (annual plan) must cover all significant aspects of the ISMS/security program over the audit cycle, with higher-risk areas audited more frequently. Auditor independence is a fundamental requirement: the internal auditor must not audit activities they are responsible for. The security manager cannot audit their own security controls. Options: rotate audit responsibilities among qualified team members (auditor A audits B''s controls, auditor B audits A''s controls), use a separate internal audit function, or commission an external party for the internal audit role.

Audit planning: for each internal audit, produce an audit plan. Audit scope: which specific controls, policies, or ISMS clauses are being audited in this engagement? Audit criteria: against which standard, policy, or procedure will conformity be assessed? (ISO 27001 Clause 6.1.2, SOC 2 CC6, or the organization''s Access Control Policy.) Audit methods: interviews (conversation with control owners to understand how the control works in practice), observation (watching a process being executed — attending a change advisory board meeting to observe the change management process), documentation review (examining policies, procedures, and records for completeness and currency), technical testing (querying the IdP for MFA enrollment rates, reviewing firewall change logs). Audit team: who will conduct this audit? What is their relevant expertise?

Fieldwork execution: the most important principle is to collect evidence, not accept assertions. When you ask a control owner "is access reviewed quarterly?" and they say "yes," that is an assertion. When you request the access review completion records from the last four quarters and verify that all reviews were completed within the defined window, with manager sign-off — that is evidence. Interview questions should be open-ended and designed to reveal actual process rather than prompt yes/no responses: "Can you walk me through the last time you conducted an access review? What did you do? What did you find? What happened with the results?"

Findings classification: Conformity (the control is designed and operating as required — document as evidence of good practice), Nonconformity Minor (a single instance of a requirement not being met, without a systemic pattern — requires corrective action but does not indicate a program failure), Nonconformity Major (a complete failure of a requirement, or multiple minor nonconformities in the same area indicating systemic failure — most serious finding, requires immediate management attention), Opportunity for Improvement (observed weakness that does not yet constitute a nonconformity but could develop into one — recommend proactive remediation).

Audit report structure: executive summary (overall audit conclusion, key findings count by severity, most significant findings highlighted), audit scope and methodology, detailed findings (each with description, evidence reference, root cause analysis, risk implication, recommended corrective action and deadline), and a corrective action plan tracking table. Corrective action management: assign each finding to an owner with a specific remediation deadline, track progress via a shared corrective action register, verify closure with evidence (not just closure assertion — "we fixed it" requires evidence that it is fixed), and report CAP status to management monthly.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 1;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'External auditors think differently from internal teams. Learn the auditor mindset, how design vs operating effectiveness testing works, what the most common audit findings are, and how to prepare your team for fieldwork.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'External Audit Preparation — The Auditor''s Mindset & What They Actually Test',
      'description', 'External auditors think differently. Learn the auditor mindset, design vs operating effectiveness testing, the most common audit findings, and how to prepare your team for fieldwork.',
      'transcript', 'External auditors — whether conducting a SOC 2 audit, ISO 27001 certification audit, PCI DSS QSA assessment, or regulatory examination — are trained professionals whose job is to provide independent assurance that your controls are operating as claimed. Understanding how they think and what they are specifically looking for is the most valuable preparation you can do before an audit engagement begins.

The auditor''s two fundamental questions for every control: Is it designed right? (Design effectiveness) Does it actually work as designed, consistently, over time? (Operating effectiveness) Type I assessments evaluate design only. Type II assessments — SOC 2 Type II, ISO 27001 Stage 2, PCI DSS ongoing assessments — evaluate both. A control that is perfectly designed but never operated (the policy exists but nobody follows it) will fail operating effectiveness testing. A control that is consistently operated but is not designed to meet the stated requirement will fail design effectiveness testing.

How auditors select samples: for operating effectiveness testing, auditors do not review every instance of a control''s operation. They apply statistical sampling. The general principle: for controls that operate frequently (daily backups — 365 instances per year), auditors test 25–60 samples. For controls that operate quarterly (access reviews — 4 instances per year), auditors test all 4 instances. For controls that operate annually (risk assessment — 1 instance per year), auditors test the one instance. Exception rate matters: if 2 of 25 tested samples fail (8% exception rate), that is a significant finding — auditors extrapolate from sample to population and conclude that 8% of all instances in the review period may have failed.

Most common external audit findings by category: Access Control — terminated user accounts not disabled within the policy-defined SLA (auditor pulls HR termination dates and compares to account disable dates), access review not completed on schedule (auditor requests evidence of the quarterly access review conducted in Q3 and finds none), user with excessive permissions not justified in access review (auditor tests specific access decisions and finds no business justification documented). Change Management — production changes implemented without completing the change approval process (auditor samples change tickets and finds instances where deployment records predate approval records), emergency changes not retrospectively approved per the emergency change procedure. Vendor Risk — required annual vendor assessments not completed for Tier 1 vendors (auditor requests assessment completion dates for critical vendors and finds overdue assessments), vendor contracts missing required DPA or BAA. Logging and Monitoring — critical systems not sending logs to SIEM (auditor requests log coverage documentation and identifies gaps), log retention shorter than required period.

Audit preparation best practices: conduct a pre-audit readiness review using the auditor''s criteria (walk through every CC category for SOC 2, every clause for ISO 27001), fix identified gaps before the audit window opens, prepare your control owners for auditor interviews (coach them to answer factually and specifically, not to volunteer information beyond what is asked, and to escalate difficult questions rather than guessing), organize all evidence in advance (do not make the auditor wait for evidence that should be readily available), and designate a single audit coordinator who manages all auditor requests, tracks PBC list status, and routes questions to the appropriate technical owners.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 2;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Evidence organization determines audit outcomes. Build a control-to-evidence mapping system, master sample testing methodology, and design a gap remediation process that closes findings before they become audit observations.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Evidence Mapping, Control Testing & Gap Remediation Tracking',
      'description', 'Evidence organization determines audit outcomes. Build control-to-evidence mapping, master sample testing, and design gap remediation tracking that closes findings before they become observations.',
      'transcript', 'Evidence organization is the operational discipline that determines whether your audit experience is smooth or chaotic. Organizations with strong evidence management fulfill auditor requests in hours, demonstrate confidence in their control environment, and produce clean audit reports. Organizations with poor evidence management scramble for weeks to respond to PBC lists, find gaps during fieldwork that should have been identified months earlier, and receive audit findings that could have been remediated with advance preparation.

Control-to-evidence mapping: the foundational artifact is a spreadsheet (or GRC platform entry) for each control that maps: the control description, the TSC criterion or framework clause it addresses, the evidence type required (configuration screenshot, training completion report, access review completion record), the evidence location (S3 bucket path, SharePoint folder, Vanta control link), the evidence period covered (for Type II — does this evidence cover the entire review period?), the last refresh date, the owner responsible for keeping it current, and the status (green: complete and current, yellow: needs refresh, red: missing or insufficient). Review this mapping monthly during the SOC 2 or ISO 27001 review period to ensure evidence remains current.

Evidence period coverage: the most common Type II audit evidence gap is a period where control operation is not evidenced. If your SOC 2 review period is January 1 – December 31, and your access review evidence covers January, April, and October (three of four quarterly reviews), auditors will note that Q3 (July review) has no evidence. They will test Q3 by requesting the Q3 access review records — if you cannot produce them, that is a finding. For high-frequency controls (daily backups, weekly vulnerability scans), ensure evidence spans the full review period, not just the most recent instance.

Control testing: before the external audit, internally test your highest-risk controls using the same sampling methodology auditors apply. Pull a sample of terminated user accounts from the last quarter and verify each was disabled within the policy SLA. Pull a sample of production change tickets and trace each to its approval record and deployment record. Pull a sample of access review completions and verify manager sign-off dates and that identified exceptions were addressed. Testing before the audit identifies gaps while there is still time to remediate — a control failure found internally can be corrected; a control failure found by an external auditor becomes an audit finding.

Gap remediation tracking: when gaps are identified — either through internal testing, compliance platform alerts, or internal audit findings — manage remediation systematically. Each gap needs: a unique tracking ID, a clear description of the gap (what is the control requirement? what is the current state? what is the gap?), a risk rating (what is the audit risk if this gap is not closed before the audit?), a remediation owner (who is accountable for closing this gap?), a remediation plan (what specific actions will close the gap?), a target closure date, and a verification method (what evidence will demonstrate the gap is closed?). Weekly gap remediation reviews, chaired by the compliance lead, drive accountability and ensure priority items receive the attention they need. The goal: zero audit-material gaps at the time the audit window opens.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 3;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Security dashboards communicate program effectiveness to executives and boards. Learn the KPIs that matter, data visualization principles for security metrics, and building dashboards that drive investment decisions.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Executive Security Dashboard Design — The Metrics That Move Decisions',
      'description', 'Security dashboards communicate program effectiveness to executives. Learn the KPIs that matter, security data visualization, and building dashboards that drive investment and policy decisions.',
      'transcript', 'An executive security dashboard translates the operational complexity of a security program into a clear, concise view of organizational security posture. Executives need to answer three questions quickly: are we secure enough? are we improving? what decisions do you need from me? A well-designed dashboard answers all three in 2 minutes without technical expertise.

Dashboard design principles: maximum 10 data elements (cognitive load — more than 10 elements and executives stop reading), always include trend data (current status without historical context is unactionable — is 67% compliance good or bad? it depends on whether it was 45% last quarter or 80%), use red/yellow/green status indicators aligned to defined thresholds (executives need immediate pattern recognition, not careful number analysis), translate technical metrics into business terms wherever possible (not "127 unpatched CVEs" but "127 unpatched vulnerabilities, 8 of which affect systems that process customer payment data"), and include one forward-looking indicator (what is the risk we are managing in the next 90 days?).

Metric selection: not all metrics are equally valuable for executive communication. Leading indicators (predictive of future security posture): MFA enrollment rate (currently 94%, target 100%), vulnerability remediation SLA compliance (what percentage of Critical CVEs were patched within the 15-day SLA in the last 30 days?), phishing simulation click rate (trending down from 12% six months ago to 7% today — security awareness training is working), patch compliance rate by business-criticality tier. Lagging indicators (measuring past performance): security incident count by severity over the last quarter, MTTD and MTTR trending, control failure rate in internal audit. Compliance status metrics: percentage of controls passing by framework (SOC 2: 94% green, ISO 27001: 89% green), next audit date, and any overdue remediation items from previous audits.

Data sources and automation: pull dashboard data from authoritative systems via API, not from manual compilation. SIEM for incident counts and MTTD/MTTR. EDR for endpoint protection coverage and vulnerability data. IdP (Okta/Azure) for MFA enrollment rates. Compliance platform (Vanta/Drata) for control status percentages. Vulnerability scanner for patch compliance. Phishing simulation platform for click rates and training completion. Automating data collection eliminates the weekly manual effort of dashboard refresh and ensures accuracy — manual dashboards contain errors due to data entry mistakes and go stale between updates.

Visualization tools: for internally managed dashboards, PowerBI and Tableau provide the most flexibility for complex security datasets. Grafana (open-source) with security data sources (Elasticsearch for SIEM data, Prometheus for infrastructure metrics) provides excellent real-time operational dashboards. For compliance-focused executives, Vanta and Drata provide built-in compliance dashboards that are automatically populated and audit-shareable. Google Looker Studio is a free option for organizations already using Google Workspace with basic dashboard needs.

Dashboard cadence: weekly operational dashboard for security leadership (detailed, operational metrics), monthly dashboard for CISO and senior management (trend analysis, program health), quarterly dashboard for executive team and legal (compliance status, top risks, program highlights, budget performance), and annual board dashboard (strategic risk posture, year-over-year improvement, investment justification). The quarterly and annual dashboards form the evidence trail for governance oversight — they demonstrate that the board and executive team have been informed of security risks and have exercised appropriate oversight.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 4;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Board-level security presentations are a career-defining skill. Learn the 10-slide maximum framework, how to translate risk into financial language, security committee structure, and the ''newspaper test'' for board communication.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Board-Level Risk Presentations & Security Committee Communication',
      'description', 'Board-level security presentations are a career-defining skill. Master the 10-slide framework, financial risk translation, security committee structure, and presenting breach scenarios to boards.',
      'transcript', 'Presenting security risk to a board of directors is one of the highest-leverage skills a security leader develops. The board has ultimate governance responsibility for the organization''s risk posture, but board members are generalists — their expertise is in business strategy, finance, and governance, not information security. Your job is to give them what they need to fulfill their oversight responsibility, in language they understand, in the time they have available.

Board attention economics: a board meeting covers 10–15 agenda items in 6–8 hours. Your security slot is typically 20–30 minutes, including Q&A. You cannot present a technical security briefing in 20 minutes and expect the board to absorb and act on it. You must ruthlessly prioritize: what are the 3–5 things the board must understand from this presentation? Everything else is supporting material in the appendix.

The 10-slide maximum framework: Slide 1 — Security posture headline. One sentence: "Our security posture improved from 61 to 74 on the industry benchmark this quarter, driven by MFA deployment completion and vulnerability remediation program maturity." Give the board a clear anchor before presenting details. Slide 2 — Risk landscape: what are the top 3 external threats most likely to affect organizations like ours? (This contextualizes everything that follows — board members read the news and are aware that ransomware attacks are prevalent; acknowledge that context.) Slide 3 — Our top 3 risks in business terms: each risk presented as potential financial impact, likelihood, and current treatment status. Not "we have a CVSS 9.2 vulnerability on a legacy server" but "a critical vulnerability in our legacy order processing system could allow unauthorized access to customer payment data, representing an estimated $4.2M breach cost exposure. We have scheduled remediation for Q2." Slide 4 — What we accomplished this quarter: 3–4 specific achievements with measurable outcomes. "Completed MFA deployment to 100% of accounts (up from 72%). Reduced MTTD from 18 hours to 6 hours. Completed SOC 2 Type II renewal audit with zero material findings." Slide 5 — What we need from the board: the decision or resource ask. "We are requesting approval for a $180K investment in a PAM solution to eliminate the privileged credential risk identified in the Q3 risk register. Without this investment, privileged account compromise risk remains at High for the next 12 months." Slides 6–10: supporting details for questions (incident details, compliance status, metrics trends, program roadmap, budget summary).

Financial risk framing: the most powerful transformation in board communication is converting security risks into financial terms. Risk quantification using FAIR model outputs: "The FAIR model analysis of a ransomware event affecting our production systems produces a 90th percentile annual loss estimate of $3.8M, with a 12% annual probability. The proposed backup architecture upgrade reduces expected annual loss to $900K at a one-time cost of $240K — a 3.4x ROI." Even simplified estimates are more actionable than severity ratings: "This risk is rated High" tells the board nothing. "This risk represents an estimated $2M–$8M exposure" gives them a basis for investment decisions.

The newspaper test: when drafting every security communication, ask: "If this incident occurred and was reported on the front page of the Wall Street Journal, would our board be able to demonstrate that they had been informed of this risk and exercised appropriate oversight?" If the answer is no — if the risk was never presented to the board in terms that would enable governance — that is a fiduciary failure. The newspaper test drives the board communication agenda: the risks that would make headlines if they materialized are the risks that must be in every quarterly board report, with current status and treatment plan.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 5;

-- ─── MODULE 17: Security Leadership & CISO Track ─────────────────────────────

  SELECT id INTO v_ch FROM public.chapters
    WHERE course_id = v_cid ORDER BY order_index LIMIT 1 OFFSET 16;
  IF v_ch IS NULL THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'M17 chapter not found'; END IF;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'The modern CISO operates at the intersection of technology, business, and risk. Master CISO responsibilities, board reporting, organizational structure, and the two competing reporting models that define the role.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'The CISO Role — Responsibilities, Authority, Board Reporting & Organizational Structure',
      'description', 'The modern CISO operates at the intersection of technology, business, and risk. Master CISO responsibilities, board reporting, organizational structures, and the reporting models that define the role.',
      'transcript', 'The Chief Information Security Officer is the executive responsible for defining, implementing, and managing the organization''s information security strategy. The CISO role has evolved dramatically over the past decade: from a technical function focused on firewall management and patch compliance to a business leadership function that manages risk, communicates with boards, influences business strategy, and builds organizational culture. The CISOs who struggle are those who remain technical specialists. The CISOs who thrive are those who become business executives who happen to have deep security expertise.

CISO core responsibilities: security strategy (defining the multi-year vision for the security program, aligned to business objectives and the threat landscape), risk management (maintaining the enterprise risk register, advising the board and executive team on risk posture), governance and compliance (ensuring the organization meets its regulatory obligations and contractual commitments), security operations (oversight of the SOC, incident response, vulnerability management, and threat intelligence programs), security architecture (ensuring new systems and services are designed with appropriate security controls), security culture (building an organization-wide culture of security awareness and responsible behavior), and budget and resource management (securing appropriate investment for the security program and allocating it to highest-impact initiatives).

CISO reporting models: two structures dominate. CISO → CTO: the CISO reports to the Chief Technology Officer, positioning security as a technology function. Advantage: close alignment with engineering and product decisions, faster implementation of security controls in technology systems. Disadvantage: potential conflict of interest (the CTO is evaluated on speed of delivery; the CISO may be pressured to compromise security standards to accelerate product delivery). CISO → CEO/CFO/CLO: the CISO reports to the business, positioning security as a business risk function. Advantage: independence from technology delivery pressures, direct access to executive decision-making, clearer separation between security oversight and technology delivery. Disadvantage: potential for less technical alignment and slower implementation when security requires engineering resources. The trend: increasingly, CISOs report directly to the CEO or into the General Counsel function, particularly in regulated industries where security failures carry significant legal liability.

Board relationship: the CISO''s board relationship is one of the most important governance mechanisms in the organization. CISOs typically present to the board''s Audit and Risk Committee quarterly, with a full board annual briefing. The Audit Committee chair is typically the CISO''s most important board relationship — this person bridges the CISO''s risk communication and the board''s governance oversight role. CISOs who invest in educating their Audit Committee chair between meetings (brief phone calls, relevant news sharing, early warning on emerging risks) build the trust that results in board support when budget requests or policy decisions require board approval.

CISO stakeholder ecosystem: CEO (business risk framing — the CEO needs to understand security risk in terms of revenue, reputation, and strategic objectives), CFO (budget and ROI — every security investment must be justified in financial terms), CLO/General Counsel (legal and regulatory exposure — the GC is a critical partner for breach notification decisions, litigation hold management, and regulatory response), CTO/CIO (technology alignment — security controls must be implementable within the technology environment), and business unit leaders (risk tolerance negotiation — each business unit has different risk appetite, and the CISO must understand and respect those differences while maintaining minimum security standards).'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 1;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'A security team is only as effective as its people. Learn how to structure a security organization, hire for mindset over credentials, build a development culture, and retain talent in the most competitive market in technology.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Building & Managing a Security Team — Hiring, Structure, Culture & Retention',
      'description', 'A security team is only as effective as its people. Learn to structure a security organization, hire for mindset, build development culture, and retain talent in the most competitive market in tech.',
      'transcript', 'The cybersecurity talent shortage is the defining challenge of security leadership. ISC² estimates 3.5 million unfilled cybersecurity positions globally, a gap that has persisted and grown for a decade. Competition for experienced security professionals is intense — qualified candidates routinely receive 3–5 competing offers. Building and retaining a high-performing security team requires deliberate hiring philosophy, intentional culture design, and competitive total compensation.

Security team organizational structure: the functional security team has four capabilities. Governance, Risk, and Compliance (GRC): GRC analysts, compliance managers, privacy officers — responsible for policy, risk registers, audit management, regulatory compliance, and vendor risk. Security Operations: SOC analysts (Tiers 1–3), threat hunters, SIEM engineers, incident responders — responsible for detection, investigation, and response. Security Architecture: security architects, cloud security engineers, identity architects — responsible for designing security into new systems and providing architectural guidance to engineering teams. Application Security and Engineering: AppSec engineers, DevSecOps engineers, penetration testers — responsible for securing the software development lifecycle and conducting security assessments. In smaller organizations, individuals may cover multiple functions; at scale, each function becomes a dedicated team.

Hiring philosophy: the talent shortage means that hiring for existing skills only severely limits the candidate pool. The most effective security leaders hire for: intellectual curiosity (will this person keep learning as the threat landscape evolves?), analytical thinking (can they work through a complex problem systematically?), communication skills (can they explain security concepts to non-technical stakeholders?), ethical judgment (will they make sound decisions under pressure?), and collaborative instincts (will they work effectively with engineering, legal, and business teams?). Then invest in training for specific technical skills. A curious, thoughtful generalist who becomes a capable security analyst in 18 months is a better hire than a technically skilled specialist who cannot communicate with business stakeholders.

Alternative talent pipelines: given the shortage of traditional cybersecurity candidates, successful security leaders build multiple talent pipelines. Internal development: identify high-performing IT generalists who show security interest and sponsor their development into security roles. Military veterans: veterans with intelligence, signals, or cyber operations backgrounds bring exceptional analytical skills, mission focus, and discipline. Career changers: professionals from legal, audit, risk management, finance, or engineering backgrounds bring domain expertise that pure security professionals lack. Apprenticeship programs: structured entry-level programs that provide real-world experience with senior mentorship — growing your own pipeline rather than competing for senior talent exclusively.

Security team culture: the cultural factors that most strongly correlate with security team retention are psychological safety (team members feel safe reporting mistakes and near-misses without punishment — essential for learning from incidents), blameless post-mortems (incident reviews focused on systemic improvement rather than individual blame), continuous learning investment (conference attendance, certification sponsorship, dedicated learning time — security professionals who are not learning are falling behind), visible career progression (defined career ladders, transparent promotion criteria, regular development conversations), and mission connection (team members should understand and believe in the security program''s importance to the organization and its customers).

Compensation benchmarking: security salaries have increased dramatically. As of 2025, US market rates: SOC Analyst Tier 1 ($65K–$90K), SOC Analyst Tier 2 ($90K–$130K), Security Engineer ($120K–$170K), Cloud Security Engineer ($140K–$200K), Security Architect ($160K–$220K), CISO at mid-market ($220K–$350K), CISO at enterprise ($350K–$600K+). Annual benchmarking against Radford, Levels.fyi, and industry-specific surveys is necessary to maintain competitive compensation. Underpaying security talent, even slightly, accelerates turnover — the cost of replacing an experienced security engineer (recruiting, training, ramp-up time, productivity loss) typically exceeds 12 months of their salary.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 2;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'Security program strategy is how a CISO turns vision into results. Learn multi-year roadmap design, budget justification using FAIR and ROSI, benchmarking against industry peers, and securing executive buy-in.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Security Program Strategy, Budget Justification & ROI Communication',
      'description', 'Security program strategy turns vision into results. Learn multi-year roadmap design, FAIR-based budget justification, ROSI calculation, industry benchmarking, and securing executive buy-in.',
      'transcript', 'A security program strategy is the multi-year plan that describes where the security program is going, why, and how it will get there. Without a documented strategy, security spending is reactive — funds are allocated to whatever problem is most urgent today, with no coherent progress toward a defined end state. With a strategy, every investment decision can be evaluated against the same question: does this advance us toward our strategic objectives?

Security program strategy structure: a three-year security strategy should answer five questions. Where are we today? (Current state assessment — what is the current risk posture, what capabilities exist, where are the critical gaps?) Where do we need to be? (Target state — what security capabilities does the organization need to achieve its business objectives and manage risk to an acceptable level?) What is the gap? (Gap analysis — which capabilities must be built, enhanced, or decommissioned?) How will we get there? (Roadmap — what investments, in what sequence, over what timeline?) How will we measure progress? (Metrics and milestones — what will demonstrate that the strategy is being executed successfully?)

Budget structure: security budgets typically follow the 40/30/20/10 model. People (40–50% of total): salaries, benefits, and contractor costs for the security team. Technology (30–40%): security tooling licenses (SIEM, EDR, PAM, CSPM, compliance automation) and infrastructure costs. Services (10–20%): managed security services, external penetration testing, audit fees, consulting. Training and development (5–10%): conference attendance, certification sponsorship, learning platforms.

Budget justification using financial risk framing: the most effective security budget presentations translate security investment into risk reduction in financial terms. Framework: Current Risk Exposure — quantify the expected annual loss for the top 3–5 risks using FAIR model or simplified expected value calculation. Proposed Investment — what specific capabilities will the investment provide? Expected Risk Reduction — how much does the investment reduce the expected annual loss of the identified risks? ROI calculation: if an investment costs $200K annually and reduces expected annual loss from $1.5M to $400K, the risk reduction value is $1.1M annually, producing a 450% annual ROI. Present this alongside the alternative: "If we do not make this investment, we accept a $1.1M expected annual loss exposure that exceeds the investment cost by 5.5x."

ROSI (Return on Security Investment): ROSI = (Risk Reduced × Asset Value) − Control Cost. The challenge: quantifying "risk reduced" requires estimating both the probability of a loss event and its magnitude. FAIR model provides the most defensible framework for these estimates, using calibrated probability ranges rather than point estimates. Even simplified estimates are valuable: the act of quantifying expected loss forces the conversation from "this is a security best practice" (which justifies nothing) to "this control reduces our expected annual loss by $X" (which justifies investment against a financial standard).

Industry benchmarking: Gartner recommends security spending of 5–15% of the IT budget, varying significantly by industry (financial services and healthcare at the high end, retail and manufacturing at the lower end). The Ponemon Institute''s Cost of a Data Breach Report provides breach cost data by industry — useful for calibrating expected loss estimates and contextualizing security investment against the alternative. CISO benchmarking surveys (Gartner CISO Effectiveness Survey, ESG/ISSA CISO survey) provide data on program maturity, team size, and budget allocation across peer organizations.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 3;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'A 3-year security technology roadmap and C-suite vendor management distinguish security operators from security leaders. Learn roadmap design, RFP methodology, contract negotiation, and technology consolidation strategy.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Security Technology Roadmap & Vendor Management at the C-Suite Level',
      'description', 'A 3-year security technology roadmap distinguishes security leaders. Learn roadmap design, RFP methodology, vendor scoring, contract negotiation, and technology consolidation strategy.',
      'transcript', 'The average enterprise security organization manages 45 or more security tools — each solving a specific problem, each requiring integration, maintenance, and expertise. This tool sprawl is expensive, creates integration gaps that attackers exploit, and requires more operational expertise than most security teams have. A security technology roadmap provides the structured approach to building the right toolset, retiring redundant tools, and managing vendor relationships strategically.

Technology roadmap structure: a three-year security technology roadmap covers five security pillars. Identity Security: current state (which IAM, PAM, and IGA solutions are deployed?), gaps (is PAM deployed for all privileged accounts? is IGA managing the full identity lifecycle?), roadmap (Year 1: deploy PAM for Tier 1 privileged accounts; Year 2: expand IGA to automate JML process; Year 3: implement phishing-resistant MFA for all users). Cloud Security: current CSPM coverage, container security gaps, planned cloud expansion. Data Security: data classification tool deployment, DLP implementation, encryption coverage. Endpoint and Network Security: EDR coverage completeness, network detection, ZTNA migration from VPN. GRC and Compliance: compliance automation platform, risk management platform, security metrics platform.

Vendor selection methodology: RFP (Request for Proposal) process: define requirements (functional requirements from the security program strategy, technical requirements from the environment architecture, operational requirements from the team that will use the tool), issue RFP to 3–5 qualified vendors, evaluate responses using a weighted scoring matrix (security effectiveness: 30%, integration capability: 25%, operational simplicity: 20%, vendor support and roadmap: 15%, total cost: 10%), conduct proof of concept evaluations in your actual environment, check references from similar-sized organizations in similar industries.

Contract negotiation at the C-suite level: CISO-level vendor negotiations differ from procurement-level negotiations. CISOs negotiate: SLAs with financial penalties for breach (vendor''s MTTR for critical incidents, uptime guarantees backed by service credits), data security addenda that extend security obligations appropriate to the sensitivity of data the vendor accesses, right-to-audit provisions for critical security tools that have privileged access to your environment, transparency commitments (advance notification of significant product changes that affect security posture, disclosure of material security incidents involving the vendor''s own systems), and multi-year pricing with defined renewal terms that prevent vendor pricing leverage at renewal.

Technology consolidation strategy: platform consolidation reduces tool sprawl, integration complexity, and total cost while improving efficacy through deeper integration. Three major security platform consolidations that large organizations are executing: Microsoft Security platform (Defender for Endpoint + Defender for Cloud + Sentinel + Entra ID + Purview — consolidating SIEM, EDR, CSPM, identity, and data security into one integrated platform for Microsoft-centric environments), Palo Alto Networks Cortex (XDR + XSOAR + Prisma Cloud + Cortex Xpanse — consolidating XDR, SOAR, CNAPP, and attack surface management), CrowdStrike Falcon platform (Falcon Prevent + Insight + Discover + Identity Protection + LogScale SIEM — consolidating EDR, threat intelligence, identity protection, and SIEM). The consolidation decision requires careful analysis: the operational savings (fewer vendor relationships, fewer integration points, reduced training burden) must outweigh the risks of concentration (single vendor dependency, migration disruption, potential capability degradation during transition).'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 4;

  UPDATE public.videos SET
    description = 'When a major breach hits the front page, the CISO''s crisis leadership determines outcomes. Master breach communication, regulatory notification obligations, board management during a crisis, and reputation recovery.',
    translations = jsonb_build_object('en', jsonb_build_object(
      'title', 'Crisis Leadership — Communicating During a Major Breach & Reputation Management',
      'description', 'When a major breach hits the front page, the CISO''s crisis leadership determines outcomes. Master breach communication, regulatory notification obligations, board crisis management, and reputation recovery.',
      'transcript', 'A major security breach is the ultimate test of security leadership. In the first 72 hours after a significant breach, the CISO simultaneously manages the technical response (containment and eradication), the legal response (breach notification obligations and litigation preparation), the executive and board response (keeping leadership informed and confident), and potentially the external response (regulators, media, and public communication). The CISOs who manage major breaches effectively are those who prepared for this scenario before it happened.

Crisis leadership principles: in a breach, you are making consequential decisions under conditions of uncertainty, time pressure, and external scrutiny. The principles that guide effective crisis leadership: acknowledge facts you are certain of, explicitly acknowledge what you do not yet know, resist pressure to provide certainty before you have it, maintain a single authoritative information source (one crisis command center, one factual record of what is known and when it was learned), and assign clear roles with clear decision authority (the incident commander makes operational response decisions; legal counsel makes breach notification decisions; the CEO makes external communication decisions — no ambiguity about who decides what).

Communication hierarchy during a breach: Internal communications must precede external communications. Before any public statement, the board must be briefed (the board cannot learn about a major breach from a press release), the CEO and executive team must be aligned on messaging, legal counsel must approve all communications, and the incident response team must have confirmed the factual basis for any public statement. External communication sequence: employees (they will be asked by clients, partners, and family — they need accurate information before the public does), affected customers (have a right to know if their data was exposed — direct notification via email or mail), regulators (mandatory notification under GDPR, HIPAA, SEC rules, and state breach notification laws), and media/public (controlled press release through PR team, coordinated with legal).

Regulatory notification obligations: GDPR Article 33 — 72 hours to supervisory authority. SEC Rule 8-K (public companies) — 4 business days for material cybersecurity incidents. HIPAA — 60 days to HHS; notify affected individuals within 60 days; notify media if >500 residents of a state. State breach notification laws — 45 US states have breach notification laws with varying timelines (California: 72 hours to AG for healthcare data, 30 days for most; New York SHIELD Act: in the most expedient time possible; Colorado: 30 days). Managing multiple notification timelines simultaneously requires a notification tracking spreadsheet assigning each obligation, its deadline, the responsible party, and current status — managed by legal counsel with CISO support.

Litigation privilege and forensic investigation: when a significant breach occurs, engaging outside forensic counsel and conducting the investigation under attorney-client privilege protects investigation findings from discovery in subsequent litigation. The CISO should work closely with the General Counsel to structure the investigation appropriately. Public statements: every public statement about a breach becomes evidence in subsequent litigation and regulatory proceedings. Legal review of every external communication — including social media posts — is non-negotiable.

Post-breach reputation recovery: the organizations that recover fastest from major breaches are those that demonstrate genuine commitment to remediation, not those that minimize or deny. Recovery actions: publish a detailed post-breach report describing what happened, what data was affected, what remediation was implemented, and what controls were added to prevent recurrence. Offer affected individuals meaningful remediation (credit monitoring for financial data breaches, identity protection services for identity data breaches). Engage proactively with regulators rather than waiting for enforcement action — voluntary cooperation typically results in better outcomes than forced compliance. Commission an independent third-party security assessment and share the results publicly — demonstrating that you have not only fixed the specific breach cause but reviewed and improved the broader security program.'
    ))
  WHERE chapter_id = v_ch AND order_index = 5;

